{"id": 3158093, "title": "Memory Leak in libcurl via Location Header Handling (CWE-770)", "submitted": "2025-05-22", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3158093", "excerpt": "## Summary:\nThis report details a memory leak vulnerability in libcurl that occurs when processing HTTP 3xx redirect responses containing a `Location:` header. Specifically, the memory allocated for the `Location:` header's value is not properly deallocated when the `Curl_easy` handle is reused for subsequent requests (e.g., when following redirects or in long-running applications that frequently reuse handles). This leads to a gradual increase in memory consumption, potentially resulting in a Denial of Service (DoS) due to resource exhaustion.\n\n### Statement clarifying if an AI was used to find the issue or generate the report:\nThis report was generated with the assistance of an AI. The vulnerability was identified through a combination of manual code analysis and AI-assisted debugging and proof-of-concept generation.\n\n## Affected version:\ncurl/libcurl version: **8.14.0-DEV** (Built from source on 2025-05-22)\nPlatform: **Kali Linux (x86_64)**\n\nYou can obtain your exact version information using:\n```bash\n./src/curl -V\n```\n**Steps To Reproduce**:\n**Set up the testing environment**:\n**Install necessary dependencies**:\n```\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install git build-essential autoconf automake libtool pkg-config zlib1g-dev libssl-dev libnghttp2-dev libldap2-dev librtmp-dev libssh2-1-dev libpsl-dev libidn2-dev libnghttp3-dev libsqlite3-dev libbrotli-dev valgrind python3\n```\n**Clone the curl repository**:\n```git clone https://github.com/curl/curl.git\ncd curl\n```\n**Build curl with debug symbols**:\n```\nautoreconf -fi\n./configure --with-openssl --with-zlib --with-libssh2 --with-libpsl --with-libidn2 --with-nghttp2 --with-ldap --with-brotli --enable-debug --disable-shared\nmake -j$(nproc)\nchmod +x curl-config\n```\n**Prepare the malicious HTTP server (Python PoC server)**:\n**Create a Python script named leak_server.py in the root of the curl directory**:\n```\nnano leak_server.py\n```\n**Paste the following Python code into leak_server.py**:\n```\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\nimport sys\n\nclass LeakHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        path = self.path\n        sys.stderr.write(f\"Received request for: {path}\\n\")\n\n        # Number of redirects to perform before stopping\n        max_redirects = 1000 # Adjust this value as needed for more/less leak\n\n        try:\n            current_redirect_count = int(path.split('/')[-1])\n    ", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "needs_human", "final_label": "Needs human review", "tone": "muted", "explain": "The available evidence does not settle the report. A maintainer should investigate.", "confidence": 0.7, "judge_verdict": "needs_human", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "203b4349af3e", "rev_source": "master on 2025-05-22", "counts": {"grounded": 16, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "Memory leak in libcurl when processing HTTP 3xx redirect responses with a Location header, causing gradual memory consumption increase and potential denial of service due to resource exhaustion.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "Memory allocated for the Location header's value is not properly deallocated when the Curl_easy handle is reused for subsequent requests", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The provided code confirms that Curl_req_hard_reset and Curl_req_free do not free req->location, and that Curl_copy_header_value allocates memory via malloc. However, the implementation of http_header() where the Location header is allegedly parsed and stored into req->location is not included in the provided source snippets (only its declaration at lib/http.c:106 is shown). Without seeing the allocation site, the full claim cannot be verified from the provided code alone.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/request.c", "line": 145, "note": "Curl_req_hard_reset sets req->location = NULL without freeing (master)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/request.c@curl-8_13_0", "line": 148, "note": "Curl_req_hard_reset sets req->location = NULL without freeing (release curl-8_13_0)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/request.c", "line": 171, "note": "Curl_req_free frees req->newurl and req->p.ftp but not req->location (master)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/request.c@curl-8_13_0", "line": 177, "note": "Curl_req_free frees req->newurl and req->p.ftp but not req->location (release)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/http.c@curl-8_13_0", "line": 293, "note": "Curl_copy_header_value allocates via Curl_memdup0 (malloc)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/strdup.c", "line": 114, "note": "Curl_memdup0 allocates with malloc(length + 1)", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The leak occurs in the handling of HTTP 3xx redirect responses containing a Location header", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The claim describes the leak occurring in HTTP 3xx redirect handling with Location headers. The http_header function implementation (where header parsing occurs) is not in the provided code, so the trigger location cannot be verified.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/http.c", "line": 106, "note": "Only declaration of http_header shown; implementation not provided", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "A malicious server can trigger the leak by sending HTTP 3xx responses with long Location headers that cause repeated allocation without deallocation", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The trigger claim depends on http_header() allocating and storing the Location header value into req->location. Since http_header's implementation is not provided, this attack path cannot be verified from the given code.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/http.c", "line": 106, "note": "http_header implementation not provided", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "The impact is gradual memory consumption increase leading to potential denial of service due to resource exhaustion", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "Impact claim depends on C1-C3 being verified. Since the allocation path is not confirmed in the provided code, the DoS impact cannot be assessed from the provided evidence alone.", "citations": []}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The reproduction involves using Valgrind to monitor memory allocations while following redirects with a malicious server that provides long Location headers", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "This describes reproduction steps, not a code claim. Valgrind output and server logs are not provided in the evidence.", "citations": []}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [], "key_reasons": ["Curl_req_hard_reset and Curl_req_free both set req->location = NULL without freeing the prior allocation — confirmed in both master and release curl-8_13_0.", "Curl_copy_header_value allocates via malloc (via Curl_memdup0) — confirmed.", "The allocation site (http_header function parsing Location: header into req->location) is not in the provided code snippets, so the end-to-end leak path cannot be fully verified from the given evidence.", "If the allocation occurs as described, this would be a repeated leak on each redirect (large leak), which the project policy treats as a potential security issue (DoS risk)."], "missing_info": ["The implementation of http_header() in lib/http.c (where Location header is parsed and stored to req->location)."], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report. The provided code confirms that Curl_req_hard_reset and Curl_req_free do not free req->location, and that Curl_copy_header_value allocates via malloc. However, the http_header() function implementation (where the Location header would be parsed and stored into req->location) was not included in the source snippets we reviewed. Could you point to the exact lines in http_header that assign to req->location, or provide that code section? This will let us confirm the full allocation→leak path.", "code": [{"name": "lib/request.c", "start": 75, "end": 167, "reason": "lines 100-100 cited by the report; definition of Curl_req_hard_reset", "rows": [{"n": 75, "text": "  if(result)", "notes": []}, {"n": 76, "text": "    return result;", "notes": []}, {"n": 77, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 78, "text": "  if(!req->sendbuf_init) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 79, "text": "    Curl_bufq_init2(&req->sendbuf, data->set.upload_buffer_size, 1,", "notes": []}, {"n": 80, "text": "                    BUFQ_OPT_SOFT_LIMIT);", "notes": []}, {"n": 81, "text": "    req->sendbuf_init = TRUE;", "notes": []}, {"n": 82, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 83, "text": "  else {", "notes": []}, {"n": 84, "text": "    Curl_bufq_reset(&req->sendbuf);", "notes": []}, {"n": 85, "text": "    if(data->set.upload_buffer_size != req->sendbuf.chunk_size) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 86, "text": "      Curl_bufq_free(&req->sendbuf);", "notes": []}, {"n": 87, "text": "      Curl_bufq_init2(&req->sendbuf, data->set.upload_buffer_size, 1,", "notes": []}, {"n": 88, "text": "                      BUFQ_OPT_SOFT_LIMIT);", "notes": []}, {"n": 89, "text": "    }", "notes": []}, {"n": 90, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 91, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 92, "text": "  return CURLE_OK;", "notes": []}, {"n": 93, "text": "}", "notes": []}, {"n": 94, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 95, "text": "CURLcode Curl_req_start(struct SingleRequest *req,", "notes": []}, {"n": 96, "text": "                        struct Curl_easy *data)", "notes": []}, {"n": 97, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 98, "text": "  req->start = curlx_now();", "notes": []}, {"n": 99, "text": "  return Curl_req_soft_reset(req, data);", "notes": []}, {"n": 100, "text": "